-#### WENO5 Interpolation
-A nominally fifth-order finite-difference WENO scheme that returns the numerical flux for interpolation [S1]. Given five input values $v_1=f(x-2dx), v_2=f(x-dx), v_3=f(x), v_4=f(x+dx), v_5=f(x+2dx)$, it returns an interpolated value $f(x+dx/2)$ such that $(f(x+dx/2) - f(x-dx/2))/dx = df/dx(x) + error$, where the error is fifth-order in smooth regions ($O(dx) \le error \le O(dx^5)$) [S1]. It includes a scaling factor `scale2` (default 1.0) [S1]. Introduced by Chi-Wang Shu, referenced in ICASE Report No 2001-11 and ICASE No 97-65 [S1].
+### WENO5 Interpolation
+A nominally fifth-order finite-difference WENO scheme that returns the numerical flux for interpolation [S1]. Given five input values $v_1=f(x-2dx), v_2=f(x-dx), v_3=f(x), v_4=f(x+dx), v_5=f(x+2dx)$, it returns an interpolated value $f(x+dx/2)$ such that $(f(x+dx/2) - f(x-dx/2))/dx = df/dx(x) + error$, where the error is fifth-order in smooth regions ($O(dx) \le error \le O(dx^5)$) [S1]. It includes a scaling factor `scale2` (default 1.0) and accepts five input values for the stencil [S1]. Introduced by Chi-Wang Shu, referenced in ICASE Report No 2001-11 and ICASE No 97-65 [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The family encompasses standard finite difference approximations for gradient computation. WENO5 was introduced by Chi-Wang Shu, referenced in ICASE Report No 2001-11 and ICASE No 97-65 [S1].
